  "prompt": "A student writes: 'The author uses narration, description, and cause-effect to develop her argument.' Explain what is wrong with this as an answer to a 5.C question, and rewrite it for one of the three methods.",
  "modelAnswer": "It is a spotting list. Skill 5.C is 'Recognize and explain the use of methods of development to accomplish a purpose', and the sentence does only the recognising. It says nothing about what any method achieves that another would not, and it would be equally true of a great many texts. The CED is explicit at course level that skills are not measured as mastery of a vocabulary of rhetorical terms, but as the appropriate application of such terminology in analysis. A rewrite for narration: by opening with her own night shift rather than with the statistics she later cites, the writer establishes that she is describing conditions she has lived rather than studied, so when the figures arrive the reader receives them as confirmation of testimony rather than as a claim needing verification. That names the method, the choice, and the effect on the reader, and it connects to what the argument can then do.",
