  "modelAnswer": "It costs on both rows. Row B’s Additional Notes state that “Writing that suffers from grammatical and/or mechanical errors that interfere with communication cannot earn the fourth point in this row”, so sentences a grader must reread to parse put a ceiling of 3 on a 4-point row. Row C is worse, because complexity that does not work is not neutral there but a listed failure: responses that “Use complicated or complex sentences or language that is ineffective because it does not enhance the argument” are named among those that do not earn the point — that is Q1’s wording; Q3 reads *because they do not enhance the argument* and Q2 *does not enhance the analysis*. So the strategy loses a point it might have had and forfeits the one it was aiming at. The false belief is that Row C rewards difficulty — harder words, longer sentences, a grander register. It does not. Its routes are operations on the argument: exploring a tension, articulating an implication or limitation by situating the argument in a broader context, making choices that consistently strengthen it, or sustaining a style that is vivid and persuasive. “Vivid” pairs with “persuasive”, and prose that must be decoded persuades nobody. Every one of those operations can be performed in short plain sentences.",
