  "modelAnswer": "Subordination puts one idea in a dependent clause that cannot stand alone, which demotes it to background, and leaves the main clause as the assertion. \"Although the pilot cut delays by a third, it cost more than forecast\" concedes the benefit and asserts the cost; reversing which half is subordinate produces an argument for continuing the pilot from exactly the same two facts. The CED states the underlying principle at STL-1.K — subordination illustrates an imbalance or inequality between ideas — and STL-1.J gives the balanced alternative in coordination. A reader rarely notices because the ranking does not look like a claim: no sentence says the cost matters more, so there is nothing offered for the reader to dispute, and readers also carry the final clause forward, which means the demotion has already shaped what they take into the next sentence.",
