  "modelAnswer": "Qualifying narrows scope; hedging reduces force without narrowing anything. \"Most of the increase, in the two wettest summers\" excludes every other summer and can be checked; \"in many ways\", \"somewhat\", \"it could be argued\" exclude nothing and leave the claim exactly as broad as before, only less committed. The cost is concrete: the Q1 and Q3 thesis rubrics award no point where the response does not take a position or where the position is vague or must be inferred, so a thesis built out of hedges can forfeit Row A even when the body is sound. The CED's reason for qualifying supports the distinction — CLE-1.W says effective arguments avoid absolute terms because they are part of an ongoing discourse, and CLE-1.X says a writer can press words, phrases and whole clauses into service as modifiers, narrowing what an argument claims to cover. Limit the scope, not the commitment. The test: after the change, does the sentence say something narrower that a reader could check, or just something weaker?",
