  "modelAnswer": "First, the effect is backwards: polysyndeton puts a conjunction between every item, and each one is a small stop, so the list slows and each item is weighed separately — producing relentlessness or exhaustion rather than speed. It is asyndeton, which strips the conjunctions out, that accelerates. Second, the terms may have been swapped in the first place: the diagnostic is to count conjunctions between items — none is asyndeton, one between every pair is polysyndeton, and one only before the final item is ordinary English and no device at all, so a response naming either term must first establish that the pattern is unusual. For the claim to be worth making, the passage would have to depart from ordinary usage, the departure would have to be something a competent writer could easily have avoided, and the response would have to say what the departure makes the reader do — hurry past an open-ended list, or stop at each item in turn — and how that serves the writer's purpose. Effect and purpose are what Row B pays for; the name is not.",
