  "family": "repetition",
  "def": "The same word or phrase begins several consecutive clauses or sentences. Only the opening is fixed; everything after it is free to change.",
  "example": "\"But, in a larger sense, we can not dedicate—we can not consecrate—we can not hallow—this ground.\"",
  "exampleAttr": "Abraham Lincoln, Gettysburg Address, 1863 — public domain.",
  "effect": "The fixed opening — *we can not*, which Lincoln wrote as two words — becomes a beat the reader can hear coming, so attention slides off it and onto the words that change. Here that forces the ear onto \"dedicate / consecrate / hallow\", and the three refusals stop reading as three separate statements and start reading as one act of refusal getting more absolute. The repetition also stalls the sentence: the reader is made to wait for the pattern to break, and the break — \"this ground\" — lands as the point.",
  "confusedWith": "epistrophe",
