  "exampleAttr": "Ralph Waldo Emerson, \"Self-Reliance\", 1841 — public domain.",
  "effect": "A metaphor imports a whole set of associations without arguing for a single one of them. Emerson does not claim that consistency frightens people, or that the minds in question are small; he states an identity, and a reader who takes in the sentence has already accepted the frame. That is the effect worth writing about: what the comparison smuggles in that the writer never had to defend, and what it makes it hard for the reader to say next.",
  "confusedWith": "analogy",
  "confusion": "A metaphor asserts identity and stops. An analogy sets up a correspondence with parts that map onto parts, and invites you to extend it. Test: ask \"and what corresponds to the wheels?\" If the text gives an answer, it is an analogy.",
