  "effect": "An allusion borrows a whole story, and its authority, in a handful of words — but only for readers who catch it. That selectivity is part of the effect: it flatters the audience that recognises the source and quietly marks out the ones who do not, so the sentence performs membership at the same time as it makes a claim. Lincoln's borrowed line also lets a political prediction arrive wearing the clothes of settled moral truth, which is a great deal of work for eight words.",
