    "text": "(1) The Kestrel Line has not carried a train since 2003. (2) Its right-of-way runs eleven kilometres from the old mill site to the reservoir, and the town has paid to fence it for twenty years. (3) Converting the corridor into a walking and cycling path would cost about $2.4 million. (4) Similarly, the town spends about $40,000 a year on fencing and liability insurance for a corridor that carries nothing.\n\n(5) A trail would be used. (6) Trails in comparable towns report between 30,000 and 90,000 visits a year. (7) Opponents say the corridor should be held in case rail returns. (8) That will not happen. (9) Although the corridor's rail bed, which was engineered for grades gentle enough for freight and therefore requires almost no regrading for a path, is the single largest reason the project can be built for the price quoted, this fact has not been mentioned at any public meeting. (10) The town should build the trail."
